<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jaxa of Köpenick</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Jaxa de Copnic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jaxa of Köpenick was a Slavic prince of the mid-12th century who ruled from Köpenick, now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, and issued coins there. After the death of the Hevelli prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pribislav-henry">Pribislav-Henry</a> he seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-the-bear">Albert the Bear</a> recaptured it in June 1157. His identity, sometimes linked with the Polish noble Jaksa of Miechów, is debated.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jaxa-of-kopenick.jpg" alt="Jaxa of Köpenick" width="235" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jaksa_z_Kopanicy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Twelfth-century sources name several men called Jaxa with similar careers: the son-in-law of the magnate Piotr Włostowic, the founder of the Holy Sepulchre monastery at Miechów, and the prince of the Sprevane on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a>. Gerard Labuda, following Jan Długosz, held them to be different people, but the view with most supporters today is that they were one man. Some scholars think his mother was Przybysława, sister of the Stodoran prince Przybysław Henryk.</p><p>In 1145 Jaxa married Agapia, also called Beatrice, daughter of Piotr Włostowic. Her dowry of estates in Silesia, Greater Poland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a>, centred on Miechów, made him one of the most powerful lords in Poland. Their only son died in 1158 as a hostage at the court of the Bohemian king Vladislav II.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>How he came to rule the Sprevane, in his own right or as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iv-the-curly">Bolesław IV the Curly</a>, is unknown. The chief source for his war over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-an-der-havel">Brenna</a> is the Tractatus de captione urbis <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> of Henry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, who says Jaxa came with a large Polish army, seized the city and took captives to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. After Brenna capitulated on 11 June 1157 he ruled from Köpenick, whose island castle was rebuilt in the mid-12th century. The capture is generally regarded as the beginning of the Margraviate of Brandenburg.</p><p>His bracteates, with legends read as Jaksa knez of Copnic, are virtually the only case of a West Slavic ruler naming his title in the vernacular rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. In 1162 he made a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and gained leave from Patriarch Amalric of Nesle to found a house of the Canons Regular of the Holy Sepulchre at Miechów. In 1167 he joined the rebellion against Bolesław the Curly in support of Casimir the Just.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After his son's death Jaxa founded or endowed many churches, among them the Norbertine houses of Zwierzyniec and Krzyżanowice and the Church of St. Michael in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>, completed by his widow. He most likely died in the 1170s, in 1176 according to the Chronicle of the Kraków Chapter. His principality on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a> probably lasted until around 1200, when Margrave Conrad II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> conquered it. A monument to him stands on the Schildhorn peninsula in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havel">Havel</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jaxa of Köpenick” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Slav prince, <b>Jaxa of Köpenick</b>, then seized it, but Albert recaptured it on 11 June 1157, the date traditionally taken as the founding of the margraviate, and thereafter styled himself Margrave of Brandenburg.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Pribislav-Henry named Albert the Bear his heir; on his death in 1150 Albert took Brandenburg, securing it in 1157 after a fight with the Slavic prince <b>Jaxa of Köpenick</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-the-hevelli">Principality of the Hevelli</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Miechów, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.36,20.03&amp;year=1164" aria-label="Miechów on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5008 414 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Jaxa of Köpenick"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5458 684h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5458 684h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50.36,20.03&amp;year=1164">What was here in 1164? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
